  "name": "ALPHA-1 ANTI TRYPSIN (AAT)",
  "code": "1024",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"208\" data-end=\"416\">The <strong data-start=\"212\" data-end=\"246\">Alpha-1 Antitrypsin (AAT) test</strong> measures the level of <strong data-start=\"269\" data-end=\"276\">AAT</strong>, a protein produced by the <strong data-start=\"304\" data-end=\"313\">liver</strong> that protects the lungs and other tissues from <strong data-start=\"361\" data-end=\"378\">enzyme damage</strong> (particularly neutrophil elastase).</p>\n<p data-start=\"418\" data-end=\"451\">This test is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"452\" data-end=\"811\">\n<li data-start=\"452\" data-end=\"533\">\n<p data-start=\"454\" data-end=\"533\"><strong data-start=\"454\" data-end=\"511\">Screen for or diagnose Alpha-1 Antitrypsin Deficiency</strong>, a genetic disorder</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"534\" data-end=\"623\">\n<p data-start=\"536\" data-end=\"623\">Evaluate unexplained <strong data-start=\"557\" data-end=\"590\">early-onset emphysema or COPD</strong>, especially in <strong data-start=\"606\" data-end=\"621\">non-smokers</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"624\" data-end=\"721\">\n<p data-start=\"626\" data-end=\"721\">Investigate <strong data-start=\"638\" data-end=\"667\">unexplained liver disease</strong>, particularly in infants, children, or young adults</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"722\" data-end=\"811\">\n<p data-start=\"724\" data-end=\"811\">Identify AAT phenotypes (via genotyping or phenotyping), especially in family screening</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"813\" data-end=\"953\">AAT deficiency increases the risk of <strong data-start=\"850\" data-end=\"872\">early lung disease</strong> and <strong data-start=\"877\" data-end=\"894\">liver disease</strong>, including <strong data-start=\"906\" data-end=\"919\">cirrhosis</strong> and <strong data-start=\"924\" data-end=\"952\">hepatocellular carcinoma</strong>.</p>\n<p data-start=\"993\" data-end=\"1160\">AAT is also an <strong data-start=\"1008\" data-end=\"1032\">acute-phase reactant</strong>, meaning its levels can rise in response to <strong data-start=\"1077\" data-end=\"1093\">inflammation</strong>, <strong data-start=\"1095\" data-end=\"1108\">infection</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"1113\" data-end=\"1123\">stress</strong>. Elevated AAT levels may indicate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1161\" data-end=\"1361\">\n<li data-start=\"1161\" data-end=\"1238\">\n<p data-start=\"1163\" data-end=\"1238\"><strong data-start=\"1163\" data-end=\"1196\">Acute or chronic inflammation</strong> (e.g., infections, autoimmune diseases)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1239\" data-end=\"1257\">\n<p data-start=\"1241\" data-end=\"1257\"><strong data-start=\"1241\" data-end=\"1255\">Malignancy</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1258\" data-end=\"1275\">\n<p data-start=\"1260\" data-end=\"1275\"><strong data-start=\"1260\" data-end=\"1273\">Pregnancy</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1276\" data-end=\"1308\">\n<p data-start=\"1278\" data-end=\"1308\"><strong data-start=\"1278\" data-end=\"1306\">Tissue injury or surgery</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1309\" data-end=\"1361\">\n<p data-start=\"1311\" data-end=\"1361\"><strong data-start=\"1311\" data-end=\"1361\">Use of oral contraceptives or estrogen therapy</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1363\" data-end=\"1497\">In these contexts, a high AAT is a <strong data-start=\"1398\" data-end=\"1445\">nonspecific marker of systemic inflammation</strong>, and not typically used in isolation for diagnosis.</p>\n<hr data-start=\"1499\" data-end=\"1502\">\n<p data-start=\"1504\" data-end=\"1627\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\">&nbsp;</p>",
